## 1. Runnable-Queue Scheduling, Shutdown & Batching/Fairness Audit

- **Duplicate execution / lost work:** `actor.rx.lock()` ([`designs.rs`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/designs.rs#L420)) prevents concurrent handler execution on the same actor. However, because `actor.scheduled.store(false, ...)` happens at line 442 *even when `pending > 0`*, a producer can flip `scheduled` (`false -> true`) and push `Some(id)` just before the worker's own `pending > 0` check at line 443, causing an extra wakeup handoff.
- **Shutdown safety:** In [`designs.rs`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/designs.rs#L795-L799), `ready_shutdown` sends one `None` per worker to `txs[worker % txs.len()]`. Because producers join after receiving all replies before shutdown begins, no work is lost in this benchmark, though a library backend would need drain-before-stop guarantees.
- **`native-sharded` wakeups ([`designs.rs`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/designs.rs#L452-L507)):** Using `wake: sync_channel(1)` with `try_send(())` after `tx.send(probe)` is deadlock-free (if `wake` is full, a wake token is already pending; if the worker already passed `rx[i]` in the current scan, it consumes the token on `wake_rx.recv()` and rescans).
- **Batching & fairness claims:**
  - In `ready-adaptive` ([`designs.rs`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/designs.rs#L421-L436)), `turn.elapsed() >= Duration::from_micros(50)` calls `Instant::now()` **after every single message**. For fast handlers (`iterations = 0`), fixed `ready-16` avoids per-message clock reads entirely, confounding adaptive batching with clock overhead.
  - In `hotspot128` (`iterations = 1000`, ~3 µs/msg), the 50 µs turn budget caps batches at ~16 messages anyway, making `ready-adaptive` nearly indistinguishable from `ready-16` on the hot actor.

